  "narrative": {
    "intro": "United Launch Services has $303M in federal contract obligations in FY2026 to date. United Launch Services recorded 31 federal awards so far, with an average award size of $9.8M. United Launch Services obligations are down 51.0% year over year. United Launch Services has a recent history of larger federal activity, with obligations of $633M in FY21, $1.1B in FY22, $1B in FY23, $1.5B in FY24, and $619M in FY25. United Launch Services receives most of its FY2026 to date obligations from two buyers: the Department of Defense with $237M and the National Aeronautics and Space Administration with $66.2M. United Launch Services sells work categorized under Nonscheduled Chartered Freight Air Transportation valued at $237M and Guided Missile and Space Vehicle Propulsion Unit and Propulsion Unit Parts Manufacturing valued at $66.2M in FY2026 to date. Current-year totals are partial (fiscal year to date), so the FY2026 figures reflect obligations recorded so far rather than a full fiscal year total.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "Who are United Launch Services' largest federal customers?",
        "body": "The Department of Defense is the largest buyer, obligating $237M in FY2026 to date. The National Aeronautics and Space Administration is the second buyer, obligating $66.2M in FY2026 to date. Combined obligations from those two agencies equal the full FY2026 to date total of $303M."
      },
      {
        "heading": "What does United Launch Services sell to the government?",
        "body": "Nonscheduled Chartered Freight Air Transportation is the largest market for United Launch Services in FY2026 to date, with $237M in obligations. Guided Missile and Space Vehicle Propulsion Unit and Propulsion Unit Parts Manufacturing is the second market, with $66.2M in obligations. Those two NAICS categories account for the FY2026 to date total of $303M."
      }
    ]
  },
